" (https://isl.stanford.edu/~cover/papers/paper2.pdf)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":16}); Total lines: 755\nL0@P0: IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON ELECTRONIC COMPUTERS\nL1@P0: Geometrical and Statistical Properties of Systems of\nL2@P0: Linear Inequalities with Applications in Pattern\nL3@P0: Recognition\nL4@P0: THOMAS M. COVER\nL5@P0: Abstract-This paper develops the separating capacities of fami\u0002lies of nonlinear decision surfaces by a direct application of a theorem\nL6@P0: in classical combinatorial geometry. It is shown that a family of sur\u0002faces having d degrees of freedom has a natural separating capacity\nL7@P0: of 2d pattern vectors, thus extending and unifying results of Winder\nL8@P0: and others on the pattern-separating capacity of hyperplanes. Apply\u0002ing these ideas to the vertices of a binary n-cube yields bounds on\nL9@P0: the number of spherically, quadratically, and, in general, nonlinearly\nL10@P0: separable Boolean functions of n variables.\nL11@P0: It is shown that the set of all surfaces which separate a dichotomy\nL12@P0: of an infinite, random, separable set of pattern vectors can be charac\u0002terized, on the average, by a subset of only 2d extreme pattern vec\u0002tors. In addition, the problem of generalizing the classifications on a\nL13@P0: labeled set of pattern points to the classification of a new point is\nL14@P0: defined, and it is found that the probability of ambiguous generaliza\u0002tion is large unless the number of training patterns exceeds the\nL15@P0: capacity of the set of separating surfaces.\nL16@P0: I. DEFINITIONS AND HISTORY OF FUNCTION\u0002COUNTING THEOREMS\nL17@P0: C ONSIDER a set of patterns represented by a set\nL18@P0: of vectors in a d-dimensional Euclidean space\nL19@P0: Ed. A homogeneous linear threshold function\nL20@P0: fw: Ed-- { -1, 0, 1 } is defined in terms of a parameter or\nL21@P0