L91@P1: of the dual statement, Theorem 2', can be found in\nL92@P1: Schlafli [6] and in Joseph [4]. It should be noted that\nL93@P1: most of these references are relatively obscure. A varia\u0002tion of the known proofs of Theorems 2 and 2' will\nL94@P1: therefore be provided in the first portion of the proof of\nL95@P1: Theorem 3.\nL96@P1: II. SEPARABILITY BY ARBITRARY SURFACES\nL97@P1: Many authors [8]-[12] have been concerned with\nL98@P1: separating sets of points with parametric families of sur\u0002faces. Cooper [8], [9] has been primarily concerned with\nL99@P1: the characterization of the natural class of decision sur\u0002faces for a given decision theoretic pattern-recognition\nL100@P1: problem, as well as with the complementary question\nL101@P1: of characterizing the natural class of problems for which\nL102@P1: a given family of decision surfaces is minimal complete.\nL103@P1: Bishop [13], Wong and Eisenberg [14], and Cooper\nL104@P1: [15] have been concerned with the problem of using rth\u0002order polynomial surfaces to implement truth functions\nL105@P1: (separating a dichotomy of the vertices of a binary n\u0002cube). Koford [16] and Aizerman et al. [10] have ob\u0002served that standard training algorithms will converge\nL106@P1: to a separating surface if one exists. In this section, the\nL107@P1: number of dichotomies of a set of points which may be\nL108@P1: separated by a faomily of decision surfaces will be found.\nL109@P1: This number foll ws directly from the function-count\u0002ing theorem when the family of separating surfaces and\nL110@P1: the set of points to be separated are carefully defined.\nL111@P1: Consider a family of surfaces, each of which naturally\nL112@P1: divides a given space into two regions, and a collection\nL113@P1: of N points in this space, each of which is assigned to\nL114@P1: one of two classes X' or X-. This dichotomy of the\nL115@P1: points is said to be separable relative to the family of\nL116@P1: surfaces if t